<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="VariLab.Views.RevisionHistoryView">

    <ScrollViewer HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">

        <StackPanel Spacing="6" Margin="32,20,32,24">

            <!-- ── v1.2.1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="v1.2.1  —  2026-07-15" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: The AAVSO Extended Format, Stellar Variability PNG, and Excel Report auto-exports now save into a numbered &quot;VariLab_ResultsN&quot; subfolder of the Target Directory (VariLab_Results1, VariLab_Results2, VariLab_Results3, ...) instead of directly into the Target Directory itself — the next unused number is picked automatically each time the period search runs, so files from separate runs on the same target no longer pile up together in one flat folder, and it reads clearly alongside other tools' output folders when comparing runs from multiple pipelines." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: APASS DR9 queries that legitimately find zero stars in a field (e.g. a real gap in APASS DR9's sky coverage) were being misreported as &quot;failed on all mirrors&quot; and popped a blocking confirmation dialog, even though the query had actually succeeded. A genuinely empty-but-valid response is no longer treated as a failure." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Hovering over the Target Directory field now shows the full path in a tooltip, matching TransitLab." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Comp Star Selection now checks, before doing any catalog queries, whether the resolved target position actually falls within the reference frame's plate solution. If it's off by more than a degree or three frame-widths (whichever is larger), a dialog explains the mismatch and stops — this catches a real failure mode where a FITS file's OBJECT header is stale (e.g. left over from a different target earlier in the same imaging session) even though the frame itself is correctly plate-solved and pointed somewhere else entirely. Previously this ran the full ~15-90s Gaia/APASS/VSP/GSPC pipeline before failing opaquely with &quot;0 candidates in frame&quot;." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Comp Star Selection now requires a Filter to be selected on the Data tab before it will run — proceeding without one (blank, or an unrecognized value from a FITS header that doesn't match any dropdown option) used to silently run comp-star selection against whatever filter value happened to be left over, rather than stopping to ask." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Selecting a new Target Directory on the Data tab now clears the Comp Stars, Photometry, and Results tabs' previous output (selected comps, light curve, period search, exports) — a dataset switch no longer leaves stale results from the last dataset visible until the next run overwrites them." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: The Help &gt; About window's version number was a hardcoded &quot;Version 1.0.0&quot; that had never been updated since the very first release — every subsequent version bump changed the app's actual version but never touched this label. It now reads the same AppVersion constant as the window title and footer." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: An &quot;Output Directory&quot; field on the Data tab, directly under Target Directory, controls where the VariLab_ResultsN export folders are saved. It defaults to match Target Directory whenever that changes, but stops auto-following it the moment you browse to a different folder yourself — so exports can be routed somewhere else (e.g. a shared results folder) without affecting where VariLab reads FITS frames from." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: A real photometry bug, found by testing against a dense cluster field (NGC 5139 / Omega Centauri) — the per-frame centroid refinement (a flux-weighted centroid search around the WCS-projected position) could lock onto a much brighter neighboring star whenever one fell inside its search window, silently producing a corrupted light curve that alternated frame-to-frame between the real target and the neighbor. Confirmed case: a target with a Gaia DR3 neighbor only 4.4&quot; away and ~2.2 mag brighter had ~65% of its frames pulled onto the wrong star. A frame (or an individual comp star) whose refined centroid drifts more than 6px from its WCS-projected position is now rejected rather than trusted — applied both to per-frame photometry and to the one-time reference-frame FWHM measurement that sizes the aperture for the whole run. This only matters for a target or comp star with a bright neighbor within the centroid search radius (mainly dense/cluster fields) — isolated fields are unaffected." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: Aperture photometry's net-flux calculation was clipping each individual pixel's background-subtracted value to zero before summing (Σ max(0, pixel − background)) instead of summing the raw pixel values first and subtracting the sky level once (Σpixel − N×background). The clipped version systematically overestimates flux, since it discards negative noise fluctuations while keeping positive ones — a bias that grows as SNR drops. Found by cross-checking VariLab's C# implementation directly against Geoff Stone's original Python reference code, which uses the unbiased sum-first approach; VariLab's aperture photometry now matches it. Verified on a real re-run: a well-exposed target shifted by +0.0002 mag on average (fainter, as expected, and negligible at that SNR) with zero change in frame count — the bias mainly matters for faint, low-SNR targets or comp stars." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Every export now also writes a CompDiagnostics subfolder — one CSV (JD, Mag, Weight, Airmass, Flux, PeakADU, Background, FWHM_X, FWHM_Y, FWHM_Mean) and one quick-look PNG light curve per star, the target plus every comp. Mag/Weight are the same bias-corrected per-comp values that feed the weighted-median combination; Flux/PeakADU/Background are the same aperture-photometry byproducts already computed for the main pipeline, threaded through instead of discarded; FWHM_X/FWHM_Y come from an added per-frame PSF moment measurement (cheap, not an iterative fit) — reported separately rather than as a single averaged FWHM because directional PSF elongation (e.g. from differential chromatic refraction) is washed out by an isotropic average. Filtered to the same accepted frames as the main light curve, so every file lines up frame-for-frame with the AAVSO/PNG/Excel output and with each other. Added for investigating whether an unexplained trend in a light curve is isolated to one star, shared across the ensemble, or tracks a real PSF/seeing change." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: On the Comp Stars tab, the &quot;Pipeline Log&quot; label was rendering below the log box instead of above it — both are docked to the bottom of the same DockPanel, and DockPanel stacks same-edge children in declaration order from the outside in, so the label (declared first) claimed the true bottom edge and the log box (declared second) ended up stacked above it. Swapped their declaration order so the label sits directly above the log box as intended." Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

            <!-- ── v1.2.0 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="v1.2.0  —  2026-07-14" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Exclude Transit — the Photometry tab gained an &quot;Exclude Transit...&quot; button that opens a popup light curve. Drag across the transit dip (or any range) to mark those frames excluded; drag again for another range, or over an already-excluded range to un-exclude it. Nothing is applied until Accept — Cancel discards the draft entirely. Lets VariLab separate real out-of-transit stellar-variability data from an exoplanet transit riding in the same dataset (e.g. MObs sessions that weren't scheduled around a known transit window), rather than the transit dip contaminating the variability amplitude and period search." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: The Photometry table gained an &quot;Excluded&quot; column, separate from the existing &quot;Rejected&quot; column — Rejected means a statistical 3σ outlier or a hard failure (no WCS, no usable comps); Excluded means a frame you manually marked as transit-contaminated. Both are tracked independently so it's always clear why a frame isn't counted. Excluded frames are omitted entirely from the Results tab's stats, period search, light curve, and the AAVSO/Excel/PNG exports — not flagged and kept, actually left out." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Plate Solve Required check — Comp Star Selection now scans every FITS file in the input directory for a valid WCS solution before running any Gaia/APASS/VSP queries. If fewer than half the frames have a WCS, a blocking dialog tells you to plate-solve the dataset first (Astrometry.net recommended for small/undersampled formats like MObs's 650×500, where ASTAP's quad-matching is unreliable) — there's no &quot;continue anyway&quot;, since a dataset that isn't plate-solved gives the pipeline nothing to measure. Added after a real case where only 1 of 89 frames in a directory had a WCS solution and the run silently produced a single-point, unreadable light curve instead of failing clearly upfront. An occasional bad frame in an otherwise-solved dataset still passes through — the existing per-frame Rejected path already handles that gracefully." Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

            <!-- ── v1.1.0 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="v1.1.0  —  2026-07-03" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: AAVSO Extended Format export now automatically detects a meridian (pier) flip from the FITS PIERSIDE header and adds a NOTES-field remark (e.g. &quot;Meridian flip detected (pier West -&gt; East at JD ...)&quot;) — no need to remember to document it by hand. Per AAVSO's own guidance, the underlying magnitudes/errors are never altered; the flip is only noted." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: Comp Stars tab now shows one combined table instead of separate Selected/Rejected grids — a green ✓ marks a selected comp, a red ✗ marks a rejected one, with the rejection reason in the last column." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: The Results tab's period search now runs automatically as soon as Photometry finishes — no button click required. A &quot;Re-run Period Search&quot; button remains for after adjusting the min/max period range or fold period." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: The Lomb-Scargle Periodogram plot has been removed from the Results tab and the Excel report — a single night's search is only a local diagnostic (short-timescale signals), not the actual long-period variability detection method, which happens downstream at AAVSO once many observers' data accumulate. The best period and its power are still computed and shown in the summary line." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: AAVSO Extended Format, Stellar Variability PNG, and Excel Report now save automatically to the Target Directory the moment the period search completes — no export buttons or save dialogs. The Status line reports exactly which files were saved and where. A new &quot;View Stellar Variability PNG&quot; button opens the most recently saved plot in an in-app window." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Diagnostics window (Tools menu) — a live, pausable view of the current session log, with Save Log and Load Previous Log. Ported from TransitLab so troubleshooting works the same way in both apps. Several previously-silent error paths (Comp Stars, Photometry, Data tab FITS auto-detect, auto-export) now write to this log instead of only showing a transient status message." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: Photometry accuracy — per-frame comparison and target positions are now refined to the actual local intensity centroid before measuring flux, rather than trusting the WCS-projected pixel exactly. This matters most for datasets that drift significantly frame-to-frame and/or were plate-solved with a less precise solver (e.g. ASTAP on MObs's 650×500/5&quot;/px format) — a small, consistent centering error on a small aperture was clipping real target flux every frame with no redundancy to average it out, unlike the multi-star comp ensemble." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: Photometry accuracy — the reference-frame FWHM measurement (which sizes the aperture for the whole run) now requires flux to exceed the local sky background by a noise-based threshold (2σ over a robust sky sigma), instead of just the flat median. At low target SNR, ordinary sky noise scattered across the measurement window was previously being counted as signal, inflating the computed FWHM and producing a hugely oversized aperture (an 18.5px aperture on a 2px-FWHM star was observed on a real faint MObs target, versus ~6px after the fix) — vulnerable to background non-uniformity across a wide field. Combined with the centroid fix above, corrected a real >1.2 mag systematic error on a faint target down to normal ensemble-to-ensemble scatter (~0.1-0.2 mag)." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: Comp Stars and Photometry table text reduced ~25% in size, with column headers kept a bit larger than the row values for readability. The Comp Stars table also now sits flush left under its header, full width (previously squeezed to the right of the header text due to a layout bug)." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: An animated progress bar appears on the Comp Stars tab while comp-star selection is running, matching TransitLab's Parameters tab." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: AAVSO Observer Code now persists between sessions — no need to re-type it every time." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: Decimal places tightened for readability — Comp Stars table: Mag/FWHM px/SNR/Sep (arcsec) to 2 places, BP-RP/RUWE to 4. Photometry table: JD to 4 places, Airmass/Mag/Mag Err/SNR to 2." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: Results tab cleanup — the summary line (Mean mag, Amplitude, etc.) now matches the Status line's style instead of standing out in bold; the Min/Max/Fold period fields were widened so their values are no longer clipped by the spinner buttons." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Comp Stars now shows a modal warning if Gaia DR3, AAVSO VSP, or APASS DR9 fails outright during a run (a real server/network failure, not simply &quot;no candidates in this field&quot;) — lets you cancel the run instead of silently continuing with whatever sources did respond. A run that's continued past a source failure carries a permanent &quot;⚠ continued after X failure&quot; note in the final status line, not just a transient dialog you might miss." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: APASS DR9 comp-star queries had been failing on every single run since this feature was written — the query requested Sloan g/r/i magnitude columns using the wrong names (VizieR's real schema uses a literal apostrophe: g'mag/r'mag/i'mag, not g_mag/r_mag/i_mag), so VizieR rejected every query with a 400 error regardless of filter." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: A non-exception HTTP failure (500/503/429, etc.) from an APASS or GSPC VizieR mirror was previously swallowed with zero log output, making that failure mode invisible in diagnostics. Both queries now log the actual HTTP status code and reason phrase." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: The Data tab's Filter dropdown now matches TransitLab's full filter list (29 codes, up from 11), including several — L, CBB, SZ, IJ, RJ — that already had real comp-star catalog support in the backend but weren't exposed in the UI at all." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: A severe, silent data-corruption bug in the Comp Stars and Photometry tables — their DataGrid columns defaulted to TwoWay binding (the same default WPF uses), so any row that got scrolled into view had its rounded, displayed text written back into the underlying photometry result, permanently truncating that frame's real magnitude/error down to 2 decimal places. Rows that were never scrolled into view kept their correct, full-precision values — producing light curves that looked suspiciously flat and error-free for whichever rows happened to be visible on screen. Every DataGrid column binding is now explicit Mode=OneWay." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Fix: AAVSO Extended Format exports were being rejected by AAVSO's own uploader whenever the FITS filter was L (Luminance) — L isn't a valid AAVSO filter code. Filter codes with no valid AAVSO equivalent (L, C, CBB, Rc, Ic) are now translated to their AAVSO-valid counterpart (CV, CR, R, I) specifically for the exported FILT field, matching AAVSO's own documented practice for Luminance/Clear/CBB imaging — comp-star magnitude lookups are unaffected, since those already treated these codes the same way internally." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: User Guide gained a Table of Contents — clickable in the in-app Help &gt; User Guide window (jumps straight to each section) and with matching anchor links in the standalone USER_GUIDE.md. The Appendix gained an entry for the filter-code mapping fix above, linking directly to AAVSO's filter-bands API and its own Luminance/Clear-filter submission guidance." Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

            <!-- ── v1.0.0 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="v1.0.0  —  2026-07-02" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Initial release. VariLab is a standalone stellar-variability analysis tool, built to eventually fold into a future TransitLab version. Takes a folder of pre-calibrated, plate-solved FITS frames and produces a differential-photometry light curve, period search, and AAVSO-ready exports." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Stone Method comp star selection, ported natively to C# from CompStarSelector (Geoff Stone). Queries Gaia DR3, AAVSO VSP, APASS DR9, and Gaia GSPC synthetic photometry; scores candidates on color match, magnitude, RUWE, isolation, and centrality; validates every candidate's PSF directly on the image (rejects saturated, low-SNR, outlier-FWHM, or elongated stars, replacing from a backfill pool). Magnitude priority chain: VSP → GSPC → APASS → G→V polynomial, with VSP prioritized first for AAVSO cross-observer consistency rather than raw numerical precision. Configurable ensemble size, 1–25 comp stars (default 10)." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Multi-frame differential photometry. Fixed-aperture photometry (sized once from the reference frame, held constant across the run) on the target and every comp star, re-projected through each frame's own WCS. Ensemble bias correction removes each comp's constant catalog-calibration offset before combining, so reported uncertainty reflects real measurement noise rather than inter-catalog disagreement. SNR-weighted median combination, 3σ/2-pass frame sigma-clipping." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Native Lomb-Scargle period search (generalized/floating-mean formulation, Zechmeister &amp; Kürster 2009 — the same one astropy.timeseries.LombScargle implements) and phase folding, with no Python dependency." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: RA/Dec auto-lookup. Enter a target name and resolve its sky position via AAVSO VSX, then NASA Exoplanet Archive, then SIMBAD, in that order." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Exports — AAVSO Extended Format (.txt), a Stellar Variability plot (.png) in EXOTIC's matplotlib-style look, and a four-sheet Excel report (.xlsx: Data, Comp Stars, Rejected Comps, and all three plots embedded). All filenames are timestamped so repeated exports never overwrite each other." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: User Guide and Revision History added under the Help menu." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: UI restyled to match TransitLab — same Nord color palette, fonts, tab layout, and Help menu (User Guide, Revision History, About)." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Comp Stars tab now shows a Rejected grid (candidates that failed PSF validation, with why) alongside the Selected ensemble; the Excel export gained matching Comp Stars and Rejected Comps sheets." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: Target Directory (renamed from Input Directory) now auto-reads the first FITS file's header — OBJECT fills Target Name (stripping a trailing lowercase exoplanet-letter suffix, e.g. KELT-8 b → KELT-8) and FILTER fills the Filter field. RA/Dec is looked up automatically right after Target Name is filled in." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: 'Comp Star Selection' header simplified (dropped the '— Stone Method' suffix, since the pipeline always blends Gaia + VSP + APASS + GSPC); 'Use AAVSO VSP' checkbox removed — VSP is now always queried alongside Gaia." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• Change: Results tab defaults tuned for single-night sessions — Min/Max period search range now 0.02–0.25 d, Fold Period now 0.10 d. Max period is also auto-suggested from the measured light-curve baseline (baseline/2, so at least 2 cycles are captured) each time Period Search runs, unless a manual value has since been typed." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: AAVSO Extended Format export now lists every ensemble comp star in the NOTES field — AUID for AAVSO VSP comps, Gaia DR3 source ID for comps sourced only from Gaia/APASS/GSPC (no AUID) — per AAVSO's own guidance that ensemble submissions should identify which comps were used, since CNAME=ENSEMBLE carries no per-comp detail." Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="• New: User Guide gained an Appendix listing sources for every AAVSO-related design decision in the app (magnitude priority chain, ensemble conventions, NOTES field usage, TRANS=NO, period-search algorithm choice), each linked to the actual AAVSO documentation or paper it came from." Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
